WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.863
This chapter covers inhaled nitric oxide.

2
00:00:03.863 --> 00:00:11.821
We start with what it is, how it dilates only the pulmonary vessels, and what it is approved for.

3
00:00:11.821 --> 00:00:19.286
Then its side effects: nitrogen dioxide, methemoglobin, and rebound pulmonary hypertension.

4
00:00:19.286 --> 00:00:29.622
Then the numbers for running it: the starting dose, how it is delivered, and the checklist for weaning and stopping it safely.

5
00:00:29.622 --> 00:00:35.775
We finish with the other inhaled pulmonary vasodilators, the prostacyclins.

6
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.776
Nitric oxide is a vasodilator the body makes in endothelial cells.

7
00:00:42.776 --> 00:00:46.747
Inhaled, it dilates only the pulmonary vessels.

8
00:00:46.747 --> 00:01:01.194
It raises cyclic GMP, which relaxes smooth muscle, and it improves blood flow to the areas that are ventilated, which improves ventilation perfusion matching and the PaO2.

9
00:01:01.194 --> 00:01:09.980
It causes no systemic hypotension, because hemoglobin inactivates it immediately, forming methemoglobin.

10
00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:20.625
Cylinders hold eight hundred parts per million, and the recommended dose is twenty parts per million, for up to fourteen days.

11
00:01:22.033 --> 00:01:40.147
Its approved use is in neonates with hypoxic respiratory failure, or persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn, to lower pulmonary artery pressure and raise oxygenation, along with ventilatory support.

12
00:01:40.147 --> 00:01:43.805
The deck's trick: NO equals neonates only.

13
00:01:43.805 --> 00:01:55.213
Hess adds that it is approved only for term and near-term neonates, above thirty four weeks, and that it reduces the need for ECMO.

14
00:01:55.213 --> 00:02:05.315
Off label, it is used in neonatal cardiac surgery and for hypoxemia or pulmonary hypertension after lung transplant.

15
00:02:05.315 --> 00:02:16.810
In ARDS it improves oxygenation only temporarily and does not improve survival, so it is a short-term bridge, not for prolonged use.

16
00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:20.468
In preterm infants it is not FDA approved.

17
00:02:20.468 --> 00:02:38.233
The deck says it is the preferred pulmonary vasodilator in preterm infants with persistent pulmonary hypertension and severe hypoxemia, but not to prevent bronchopulmonary dysplasia: NO for PPHN, not BPD.

18
00:02:39.667 --> 00:02:50.640
Nitric oxide combines with oxygen to form nitrogen dioxide, which is toxic to the lung, and a higher FiO2 makes more of it.

19
00:02:50.640 --> 00:03:04.468
Nitric oxide also binds hemoglobin to form methemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen, a functional anemia, and it shifts the dissociation curve to the left.

20
00:03:04.468 --> 00:03:11.872
On withdrawal, rebound pulmonary hypertension can be severe and cause desaturation.

21
00:03:11.872 --> 00:03:18.117
It is contraindicated in neonates who depend on a right to left shunt.

22
00:03:18.117 --> 00:03:29.179
The risks of methemoglobin and nitrogen dioxide rise above twenty parts per million, with no added response at higher doses.

23
00:03:29.179 --> 00:03:36.673
Monitor nitric oxide, nitrogen dioxide and methemoglobin, and cardiovascular status.

24
00:03:36.673 --> 00:03:48.717
Hess adds that the occupational limit for nitrogen dioxide is five parts per million, but lung injury has been reported at two or less.

25
00:03:50.133 --> 00:03:58.757
The deck defines methemoglobinemia as a level above one percent, with a normal range of zero to three.

26
00:03:58.757 --> 00:04:03.577
Three to fifteen percent gives slight skin discoloration.

27
00:04:03.577 --> 00:04:07.043
Fifteen to twenty percent gives cyanosis.

28
00:04:07.043 --> 00:04:14.737
Twenty five to fifty percent gives headache, shortness of breath, confusion and chest pain.

29
00:04:14.737 --> 00:04:19.979
Fifty to seventy percent gives arrhythmias, seizures and coma.

30
00:04:19.979 --> 00:04:23.868
And above seventy percent usually means death.

31
00:04:23.868 --> 00:04:41.708
Hess adds that methemoglobin is uncommon at twenty parts per million or less, that nitric oxide can also inhibit platelets, and that prolonged high doses raise the risk of kidney dysfunction, especially in ARDS.

32
00:04:43.133 --> 00:04:51.659
The recommended starting dose is twenty parts per million, and it can often be reduced quickly to five or six.

33
00:04:51.659 --> 00:04:55.302
Treatment may continue for up to fourteen days.

34
00:04:55.302 --> 00:04:59.255
The most common systems are the INOvent and INOmax.

35
00:04:59.255 --> 00:05:19.872
Storage cylinders deliver nitric oxide through an injector in the inspiratory side of the ventilator circuit, near its outlet, and a sensor measures the ventilator's inspiratory flow, so the injection rate adjusts in proportion and the delivered dose stays constant.

36
00:05:19.872 --> 00:05:30.258
It can be given by nasal cannula or mask to patients breathing on their own, but it is more commonly used with mechanical ventilation.

37
00:05:30.258 --> 00:05:38.008
The equipment limits the contact time between nitric oxide and oxygen, to keep nitrogen dioxide low.

38
00:05:39.433 --> 00:05:47.820
Benefits, a higher saturation and lower pulmonary artery pressure, may appear soon after starting.

39
00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:50.473
Stopping is the dangerous part.

40
00:05:50.473 --> 00:06:00.144
Before withdrawing, reduce the dose to the lowest effective level, ideally five to six parts per million or less.

41
00:06:00.144 --> 00:06:11.526
Make sure the patient is hemodynamically stable, and verify tolerance of an FiO2 of forty percent or less and a PEEP of five or less.

42
00:06:11.526 --> 00:06:16.489
Monitor closely, and be ready to give hemodynamic support.

43
00:06:16.489 --> 00:06:23.592
Wean in small steps of three to five parts per million at a time, to avoid rebound.

44
00:06:23.592 --> 00:06:31.637
If methemoglobin rises above one and a half percent, recommend lowering the dose as tolerated.

45
00:06:31.637 --> 00:06:39.510
If the upper airway is intact or the patient is extubated, recommend a nasal cannula system.

46
00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:49.608
Hess adds a final step: wean to one part per million for thirty to sixty minutes, and raise the FiO2, before stopping.

47
00:06:51.033 --> 00:06:52.564
Pause and decide.

48
00:06:52.564 --> 00:07:05.167
A neonate on nitric oxide is now on ten parts per million, an FiO2 of fifty percent, and a PEEP of six, and the team wants to stop it today.

49
00:07:05.167 --> 00:07:10.208
Is the patient ready, and how should the dose come down?

50
00:07:11.133 --> 00:07:17.133
[6-second practice pause]

51
00:07:17.633 --> 00:07:18.296
Not yet.

52
00:07:18.296 --> 00:07:34.445
The dose should first be at the lowest effective level, ideally five to six parts per million or less, and the patient should tolerate an FiO2 of forty percent or less and a PEEP of five or less.

53
00:07:34.445 --> 00:07:38.917
Here the dose is ten, the FiO2 fifty and the PEEP six.

54
00:07:38.917 --> 00:07:51.008
Wean the nitric oxide in small steps of three to five parts per million, keep the patient stable, and be ready for rebound pulmonary hypertension.

55
00:07:52.433 --> 00:08:04.068
Other inhaled pulmonary vasodilators are aerosolized medications, not gases: iloprost, Ventavis; treprostinil, Tyvaso; and epoprostenol, Flolan.

56
00:08:04.068 --> 00:08:15.217
They dilate the pulmonary arterial beds and decrease platelet aggregation, which reduces intrapulmonary shunting and improves oxygenation.

57
00:08:15.217 --> 00:08:30.811
They are used in spontaneously breathing patients with chronic pulmonary hypertension, and more recently in ventilated patients with acute pulmonary hypertension from acute lung injury or ARDS.

58
00:08:30.811 --> 00:08:42.768
The class is indicated for pulmonary arterial hypertension that limits activity in adults, and for persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn.

59
00:08:42.768 --> 00:08:59.170
The care plan deck adds three rules: use the manufacturer's recommended delivery device, protect staff with expiratory filters or a negative pressure room, and titrate the dose to the patient's response.

60
00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:05.633
The instructor's note: titration is extremely important, especially with Flolan.

61
00:09:07.067 --> 00:09:07.871
One more.

62
00:09:07.871 --> 00:09:13.862
A patient on nitric oxide has a methemoglobin level of two percent.

63
00:09:13.862 --> 00:09:22.267
What does the care plan deck recommend, and why does methemoglobin matter for oxygen delivery?

64
00:09:23.167 --> 00:09:29.167
[6-second practice pause]

65
00:09:29.667 --> 00:09:41.467
Two percent is above the one and a half percent threshold, so recommend lowering the nitric oxide dose as tolerated, and keep monitoring.

66
00:09:41.467 --> 00:09:54.990
Methemoglobin cannot carry oxygen, so it acts as a functional anemia, and it shifts the dissociation curve to the left, so hemoglobin holds on to its oxygen.

67
00:09:54.990 --> 00:10:00.417
The risks of methemoglobin rise above twenty parts per million.

68
00:10:01.833 --> 00:10:04.312
Here is the chapter in brief.

69
00:10:04.312 --> 00:10:18.499
Inhaled nitric oxide dilates only the pulmonary vessels, improving matching and oxygenation, because hemoglobin inactivates it before it reaches the rest of the body.

70
00:10:18.499 --> 00:10:30.122
It is approved for neonates with persistent pulmonary hypertension; in ARDS it is only a short bridge, and it is not for preventing BPD.

71
00:10:30.122 --> 00:10:35.507
Start at twenty parts per million and come down to five or six.

72
00:10:35.507 --> 00:10:44.309
Watch nitrogen dioxide, methemoglobin above one and a half percent, and rebound pulmonary hypertension.

73
00:10:44.309 --> 00:10:59.095
Before stopping, the patient needs the lowest dose, stability, an FiO2 of forty percent or less and a PEEP of five or less, weaning in three to five parts per million steps.

74
00:10:59.095 --> 00:11:04.308
The prostacyclins are inhaled aerosols that do a similar job.
